## Rhombus-bulk separator source: complete successor comparison

Sections 1–4 and 6 of source `0e49b450...` are covered by Audit 133: actual
unloaded separator components have full pinned rank and a positive row
dependence, so `t_j>=delta_j+3`; graph diameter bounds their rhombus area.
Minimum-separator essentiality saves one boundary contact at each unloaded
cut vertex. Unit-capacity vertex splitting includes both terminal sets and
yields distinct-endpoint Menger paths. The convex-face version counts only
supporting face edges in each remaining component. These arguments neither
move a source nor extend core rigidity to contact isolates.

Section 5's cap trim and complete-grid version are preserved by Audit 137
under the explicit co-located convex cap, coherent grain, linear perimeter
and deep attachment premises. Boundary-tube and height-slab estimates retain
quadratic area and linear perimeter; the cropped coherent component supplies
an actual complete grid. Returned paths have linear minimum length and
quadratic distinct internal population after trimming their initial bulk
visits. The source does not prove that an arbitrary canonical source supplies
the cap package.

The source's additional same-host/chord fork remains a conditional candidate,
outside Audit 137's promoted scope. Its exact argument is: every return stays
in the loaded force hull `F` until its first loaded endpoint. Under the stated
proper-host-arc hypothesis, the chord line through the two distinct extreme
vertices `q_0,q_1` intersects `F` exactly in `[q_0,q_1]`. If at least half of
the returns end off that host arc, their heights pass from `eta>h` to
`eta<=0`. The first passage is an actual unit contact meeting the closed
chord segment, with a source endpoint within distance one of it. Disjoint
return vertices give distinct contacts and endpoint labels. A passage may
occur at a vertex; proper transverse crossing is not claimed. Otherwise at
least half of the distinct loaded endpoints are on the host arc. This gives
no diameter edge, route payment or source replacement.

Section 7's square/near-triangular oblique grids are explicitly pinned,
nonglobal regressions. Audit 133 preserves their square-root return order;
the source's lattice displacement estimate proves its all-size separation
claim. The guaranteed connector coefficient is too small to exhaust source
population; it is a lower floor, never an upper bound on actual connectors.
Section 8 supplies no additional mathematical premise.

